    "title": "Man Convicted of Murdering University of Idaho Students Asks to Withdraw Guilty Plea",
    "text": "• Bryan Kohberger, 31, filed a hand-written petition to withdraw his guilty plea and go to trial.\n• He is serving four consecutive life sentences for the murders of Kaylee Goncalves, Ethan Chapin, Xana Kernodle, and Madison Mogen.\n• Kohberger pleaded guilty to the stabbing deaths in 2022, which scarred the close-knit community of Moscow, Idaho.\n• He claims his attorneys manipulated him into making the deal by promising better prison conditions and scaring him about life on death row.\n• Kohberger's attorneys failed to disclose or review evidence that might have helped his case, such as an \"unknown hair in Chapin's hand.\"\n• He also claims his attorneys told him his plea \"need not be truthful\" and promised him life in prison would come with \"immediate contact visits, freedom of movement, employment.\"\n• In 2022, Kohberger confirmed he was pleading guilty \"freely and voluntarily\" because he was, in fact, guilty.\n• He had to agree to waive his rights to appeal, confirm he understood the punishments, and assure the court he wasn’t coerced by his attorneys or anyone else.\n• Kohberger's plea deal included four back-to-back life sentences without the possibility of parole, plus an additional 10 years.\n• Idaho law requires a defendant to show \"manifest injustice\" to withdraw a guilty plea, which is a tough legal standard.\n• Kohberger's team of four court-appointed attorneys reached the plea agreement to save his life, as they had tried multiple times to get capital punishment off the table.",
    "summary": "Bryan Kohberger, 31, filed the hand-written petition on Monday from the Idaho Maximum Security Penitentiary where he is serving four consecutive life sentences for the murders of Kaylee Goncalves, Ethan Chapin, Xana Kernodle and Madison Mogen in a rental home near campus in 2022.",
